# Storytelling with Data — generated charts

**Triggers — treat any of these as this rule firing.** Chart · graph · plot · figure ·
visualization · exhibit · BarChart · LineChart · PieChart · canvas with series.

Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic, *Storytelling with Data*. Numbers still come from
`.cursor/rules/solving.mdc`. This rule governs how those numbers are drawn.

Write the takeaway sentence first. Then choose the visual that proves it. A
chart with no claim is decoration.

## Choose the visual

| If the point is… | Use | Do not use |
|---|---|---|
| One or two numbers | `Stat` | A chart |
| Lookup / mixed units | `Table` | Bars of incomparable units |
| Change over time | `LineChart` | Bars of a long time series |
| Categorical comparison or rank | `BarChart` (`horizontal` if labels are long) | Pie, donut, 3D |
| Part-to-whole **and** the total is the story | Stacked bar, sparingly | Pie; never stacked to compare a middle segment over time |
| Two points in time, many items | Two-column table or paired bars | Spaghetti lines |
| Relationship between two measures | Scatter (custom SVG) or a stated r with the two series | A dual-axis chart |

**Banned.** `PieChart` / donut. 3D. Dual axes. Rainbow default coloring.
`LineChart fill` unless volume of a **single** series is the point.

**Bars start at zero.** `beginAtZero` stays true on every bar chart. Line charts
may zoom (`beginAtZero={false}`) only when the story is movement inside a tight
band — and the caption must say so.

**Order with intent.** Time is chronological. Rank is descending (or the order
the story needs). Never alphabetical unless the job is lookup.

## Gray first, then highlight

Put every series in `tone: "neutral"` except the one the takeaway names. Give
that series `info` (focal), `danger` (deterioration), or `success` (improvement)
— one accent, used the same way across the whole artifact.

The canvas `BarChart` **rainbows a single series by category** unless `tone` is
set. Always set `tone`. To highlight one bar, split into two series (rest vs
story) aligned on the same categories; do not leave the default palette on.

If every series is colored, nothing is emphasized. Peers, prior periods, and
denominators stay gray.

## Declutter

Remove anything that does not make the takeaway faster to read: extra decimals,
redundant legends (label the story series in the title or an annotation), heavy
gridlines, chart borders, background fills, data markers on every point, and
category labels that repeat the title.

Directly annotate the point on the chart (the inflection, the gap, the
threshold). Use `referenceLines` for targets, means, and break-evens — not as
decoration.

One chart, one point. If you need two claims, draw two charts.

## Title is the claim

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — names the metric
<H2>Internet net adds by quarter</H2>
<BarChart
  categories={QTRS}
  series={[{ name: "Net adds (000s)", data: netAdds }]}
/>

// ✅ GOOD — states the point; gray vs focal; zero baseline; source
<H2>Internet net adds stayed negative for 10 straight quarters</H2>
<BarChart
  categories={QTRS}
  series={[{ name: "Internet net adds (000s)", data: netAdds, tone: "danger" }]}
  beginAtZero
  valueSuffix="k"
/>
<Text size="small" tone="tertiary">
  Source: Ex99.1 / trending · 2024Q1–2026Q2 · thousands of customers
</Text>
```

The reader who sees only the chart must still get the claim, the units, the
series names, and the source. Do not outsource the “so what” to a paragraph
below.

## Pre-delivery gate — do not ship a chart until all are true

1. A takeaway sentence exists, and it is the title.
2. The visual matches the table above; no pie, no dual axis, no 3D.
3. Every non-story series is `neutral`; one accent carries the claim.
4. Single-series bars have an explicit `tone` (no SDK rainbow).
5. Bars begin at zero; any zoomed line axis is disclosed.
6. Clutter is gone; the focal point is annotated on the chart.
7. Units, source, and time range are on the exhibit.
8. The chart can be removed without losing a second, different claim — if it
   cannot, split it.
